I forgot to mention that you also need to rebuild libsecure
after enabling the MLS configuration option (because the
get_user_sid function has conditional code to allow the
user to specify a MLS level) and relink the modified login
program to use the updated libsecure.  You then need to
create a /etc/security/default_context.mls file using
the same format as the /etc/security/default_context file
except for the addition of the MLS level attribute.

--
Stephen D. Smalley, NAI Labs
sds@tislabs.com


On Fri, 23 Mar 2001, Stephen Smalley wrote:

> As mentioned in the help for the CONFIG_FLASK_MLS option, we haven't
> configured the MLS policy for use yet, so we don't recommend enabling
> it.  But if you really want to do so, here is what is needed in
> addition to enabling CONFIG_FLASK_MLS in the kernel configuration:
> 
> 1) If you've previously built a non-MLS configuration, you need to
> remove the old checkpolicy program and its object files (*.cp.o) from
> kernel-2.4/security and rebuild them.  The dependencies for the
> checkpolicy object files don't get generated by make dep, so it
> doesn't automatically notice that it needs to rebuild that program
> when you change the kernel configuration. 
> 
> 2) As you realized, you need to set CONFIG_FLASK_MLS to y in
> policy/Makefile.  When you subsequently perform a 'make', if
> you haven't already created your own .mls configuration files,
> it should generate some defaults from the existing files by simply
> adding an unclassified attribute for security contexts and users.


